import TextPage from '../layouts/TextPage.astro';
---
<TextPage
  title="Methodology"
  description="Why FixedR is a framework, not a coach: the four questions, the conditions for calm process-driven trading, and the plan-versus-outcome discipline that carries you across months, not one trade."
  eyebrow="The process that keeps you here after month three"
  h1="Plan your trade, trade your plan."
  seite="Methodology"
  hinweis="Reading time · about 6 minutes"
>
    <p>
      Every trader trades according to some strategy, even the one who has never written it
      down. What isn't written down can't be broken in a way you can point to — and can't be
      improved either. It just quietly bends, trade after trade, until the record and the
      intention have nothing to do with each other.
    </p>

    <h2>The four questions</h2>
    <p>
      FixedR doesn't hand you a strategy. It holds a place for the answers to four questions,
      and keeps asking them the same way every time:
    </p>
    <div class="fragen">
      <div class="frage"><span class="kreis">01</span><span class="txt"><span class="t">What exactly is my strategy?</span><span class="h">as steps, not a feeling.</span></span></div>
      <div class="frage"><span class="kreis">02</span><span class="txt"><span class="t">Do I recognise my setup reliably?</span><span class="h">before the trade, not after.</span></span></div>
      <div class="frage"><span class="kreis">03</span><span class="txt"><span class="t">What do the numbers say?</span><span class="h">in R, comparable across accounts and time.</span></span></div>
      <div class="frage"><span class="kreis">04</span><span class="txt"><span class="t">Do I hold to it when it counts?</span><span class="h">the question a plan alone can't answer.</span></span></div>
      <span class="schluss"></span>
    </div>

    <h2>A framework, not a coach</h2>
    <p>
      FixedR doesn't hand down a process — it gives it a form. The content is yours: your
      strategy, your setups, your rules. What the software supplies is structure that any of
      that can be tested against, the same way for every strategy that walks in.
    </p>
    <p>
      The bar for a step is the <strong>oma test</strong>: if the step isn't clear enough that
      someone with no trading background could tick it off, it isn't a step — it's a feeling
      with a number attached. Results are read in neutral units (R, win rate, profit factor),
      and a strategy earns real money only after enough repetitions have said something —
      training, then backtest, then a challenge.
    </p>
    <p>
      The structural model here is the scientific method, not a competition. It doesn't tell a
      trader what to believe; it tells them how a claim has to be checked before it counts.
      Every strategy is allowed in, none is favoured, none skips the check.
    </p>

    <h2>Conditions, not a promise</h2>
    <p>
      A tool helps whoever already masters it. Conditions act on whoever moves through them —
      and that is the more honest claim to make. FixedR is built around three conditions for
      trading well, not for trading successfully:
    </p>
    <div class="bedingungen">
      <div><span class="k">What you see</span><span class="t">No money on screen — nothing to negotiate with.</span></div>
      <div><span class="k">How it's structured</span><span class="t">A process and a plan stand in for gut feeling.</span></div>
      <div><span class="k">What you learn</span><span class="t">Measurement instead of self-deception.</span></div>
    </div>
    <p>
      Calm isn't a fourth condition — it's what tends to follow once the first three hold.
      And to be precise about what that is and isn't: these conditions are necessary, not
      sufficient. They describe the trading they make possible, not an outcome they can
      promise — no structure can promise that, and you should distrust anything that does.
    </p>

    <h2>Why failure rarely sits in the analysis</h2>
    <p>
      Ask a trader who has failed an evaluation what went wrong, and the honest answer is
      almost never "my edge was wrong." It's one afternoon of tilt, one stop moved by hand,
      one revenge trade that had nothing to do with the setup. The strategy was fine on paper.
      It just wasn't load-bearing at the moment it mattered.
    </p>
    <p>
      That isn't a character flaw to be diagnosed — it's what happens to a rule that lives
      only in intention. FixedR holds the rule you wrote down; it doesn't watch you and
      it doesn't judge you for the day it slipped. It just makes the disciplined path the
      only path the software offers.
    </p>

    <h2>Two ways to spend the same stretch of time</h2>
    <p>
      One path spends a stretch of months on methodology and routine — writing the strategy
      down, testing it against the four questions, building the record that says whether it
      holds. The other spends roughly the same stretch on a series of attempts that burn out
      one after another, each one a fresh start with nothing carried over from the last. Same
      time, same effort, different thing to show for it at the end.
    </p>

    <h2>The remainder, unshortened</h2>
    <p>
      Here is the part a framework can't paper over: <strong>a bad strategy isn't saved by
      R-discipline either.</strong> Fixed risk, mandatory stops and a clean record make a bad
      edge easier to see and cheaper to find out — they don't turn it into a good one. That's
      not a caveat buried in fine print; it's the reason the rest of this is credible.
    </p>

    <span class="ln" aria-hidden="true"></span>
    <p>
      Methodology is the second floor of FixedR — the mechanics that run from day one are on
      <a href="/product">Product</a>. <a href="/waitlist">Join the waitlist</a> to get in line.
    </p>
</TextPage>
